of $F$ along $C$ can be written as\n\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr =$" 13194139535875 false] [17592186134053 1100 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x+y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186134053 1145 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩{|}_{z=10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨-2(10-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-20)\\cdot (-2x)-0\\cdot (-2y)+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071512/assets/c181e209cca01a1d6e372caa7f7810a5fa4aa9f177595bd92f937a7cba8b8a75.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371771 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371772 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371773 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371774 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371775 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1041 "selection" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371772 13194139535875 true] [17592186371770 1110 #uuid "fbf1a81a-2024-56dc-a9d2-5477498cb0c7" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371771 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371771 1111 #uuid "94dd2b94-a8ff-53e1-9e70-4ec0db0c765c" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371772 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,4{x}^{3}+4x{y}^{2}-40x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371772 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371772 1111 #uuid "598f20df-cb3c-538a-a358-7a96a145ff64" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371773 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}\\,20-4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371773 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371773 1111 #uuid "a2a944e0-6aa3-58b8-89d0-38f773be8c7f" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371774 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int