os}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371811 1113 #uuid "83c7df1b-5697-5938-b68a-ced77e0abb75" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371811 1145 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-[0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0)] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371813 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371814 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371815 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371816 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371817 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1041 "selection" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371813 13194139535875 true] [17592186371812 1110 #uuid "ad0e1b0b-7d53-5d7f-96ac-3cb8010d14e9" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371813 1034 "-\\pi " 13194139535875 true] [17592186371813 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371813 1111 #uuid "1202d2aa-d66a-5634-bd2e-399ef6d4bfaf" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371814 1034 "2" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371814 1111 #uuid "5adb85d2-3563-531e-a5d8-04aa47e96da8" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371815 1034 "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371815 1111 #uuid "fdcb8f6d-46bb-5c3f-a4f7-0f2edaea6bba" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371816 1034 "2\\pi " 13194139535875 true] [17592186371816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371816 1111 #uuid "aca4c20d-00de-5eb2-9dee-e18b66fe96e3" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371817 1034 "-1" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371817 1111 #uuid "e785b891-0cc0-5762-b698-bbf3c12a4a0d" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371818 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371819 13194139535875 true] [17592186371818 1089 "q-162361" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371818 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535875 true] [17592186371818 1100 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x-y=1,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371818 1113 #uuid "ce55f8d2-cf70-5f6b-9799-c8329a828edd" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371818 1145 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2})=4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩{|}_{z=1+{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,-x,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(-x)\\cdot (4y)+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071512/assets/d84b3ecc79c58a128d2f7ea262692d9f66dd9882ff244fb7c3dadd2b2571b550.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371820 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371821 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371822 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371823 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371824 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1041 "selection" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371822 13194139535875 true] [17592186371819 1110 #uuid "011244fc-c455-5385-82b4-0139d9eb50db" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371820 1034 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x-1}\\,-2xy+1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371820 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535875 true] [17592186371820 1111 #uuid "ccac1a52-dae7-52bc-8e8d-a0e3b920cb47" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371821 1034 "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{0}\\,-2xy-1\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535875 true] [17592186371821 1111 #uuid "798f0abe-a725-5062-b5be-