7592186045454 13194139535875 true] [17592186371831 1111 #uuid "bea4d31a-4afa-586b-9a6d-c8611ac2c59c" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371832 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371833 13194139535875 true] [17592186371832 1089 "q-162366" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371832 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535875 true] [17592186371832 1100 "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." 13194139535875 true] [17592186371832 1113 #uuid "71240cf4-ff1e-5588-9e08-c1a6960656ce" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371832 1145 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=4.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-x{e}^{z},\\,0,\\,{e}^{z}⟩{|}_{z=4} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{{e}^{4}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-x{e}^{4})-0\\cdot 0+{e}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ with radius $2$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{4}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={e}^{4}[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi {e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371834 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371835 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371836 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371837 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371838 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1041 "selection" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371835 13194139535875 true] [17592186371833 1110 #uuid "c8dd5e52-5957-522b-ad4d-1d2e44e1233a" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371834 1034 "2\\pi +{e}^{4}" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371834 1111 #uuid "0a755254-cbb1-55b4-a9e7-3ff3d1325295" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371835 1034 "4\\pi {e}^{4}" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535875 true] [17592186371835 1111 #uuid "d4df7d4c-9880-5e86-9643-4552156e5f7c" 13194139535875 true] [17592186371836 1034 "4{\\pi }^{2}+{e}^{2}" 13194139535875 true